Deployment will run in stages, so each part can be checked before the next. This message covers stage 1: the whole backend in Docker: MySQL, Kafka, the six services and the gateway, started with one command. Stage 2 adds the frontend container, one public address with HTTPS, and moving it to a server. I can’t run Docker from here, so the test table at the end is how we check it.

The Java images follow the tag pattern <java-version>-<jre or jdk>. For example eclipse-temurin:25-jre is the runtime-only Java 25 image. Use the Java version your pom.xml files declare. 
adoptium

What you end up with
LMS\
  docker-compose.yml
  .env                          (secrets, never committed)
  deploy\db\init\               (database setup scripts)
  auth-service\Dockerfile
  lottery-service\Dockerfile    (the same file in every service folder)
  ...

Only the gateway (port 8080) is reachable from outside. MySQL, Kafka and the services stay on Docker’s private network, so a mistake in one of them isn’t exposed. Services reach Kafka at kafka:19092 over that network, which also avoids the Windows port-forwarding trouble you had earlier.

Step 0: Prepare
Give Docker enough memory. Seven Java services, MySQL and Kafka need about 6 GB, so allow 8 GB. In Docker Desktop, open Settings, then Resources. With WSL2, set memory=8GB under [wsl2] in C:\Users\<you>\.wslconfig, then run wsl --shutdown and reopen Docker Desktop.
Stop everything that’s running now: all services in IntelliJ, the gateway, and the old Kafka (cd C:\lottery-infra, then docker compose down). Otherwise port 8080 is taken.
Folders and OneDrive. Your code is under OneDrive\Desktop\LMS. Docker can only read files that are really on disk, so right-click the LMS folder and choose Always keep on this device. Check the folder names of your seven projects. I assume auth-service, lottery-service, ticket-service, draw-service, winner-service, audit-service and api-gateway, all directly inside LMS. If the gateway lives elsewhere, move it there or change its path in the compose file.
Java version. Open one service’s pom.xml and read <java.version> (I’ll assume 25).
Step 1: Dockerfile (the same file in all seven service folders)
dockerfile
ARG JAVA_VERSION=25

# Stage 1: build the jar
FROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-${JAVA_VERSION} AS build
WORKDIR /build
COPY pom.xml .
RUN mvn -B -q dependency:go-offline
COPY src ./src
RUN mvn -B -q -DskipTests package

# Stage 2: a small runtime image that runs as a normal user, not root
FROM eclipse-temurin:${JAVA_VERSION}-jre
WORKDIR /app
RUN useradd --system --uid 10001 --no-create-home appuser
COPY --from=build /build/target/*.jar app.jar
USER appuser
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=70", "-jar", "/app/app.jar"]

Save it as Dockerfile (no extension) in each folder. Add a .dockerignore file in each folder as well, so your IDE files and old builds aren’t sent to Docker:

target/
.idea/
*.iml
.git/
Step 2: Database scripts (folder LMS\deploy\db\init)

The services run with ddl-auto=validate, so the tables must exist before they start. A fresh database is empty, so we create the structure from your current one.

10-schema.sql: in MySQL Workbench open Server, then Data Export. Tick the six schemas (lottery_auth_db, lottery_db, lottery_ticket_db, lottery_draw_db, lottery_winner_db, lottery_audit_db). Choose Dump Structure Only, Export to Self-Contained File, and tick Include Create Schema. Save it as 10-schema.sql in that folder. Your test data, users and password hashes stay out of it.

20-roles.sql: the four roles are the only data the system needs to start:

sql
USE lottery_auth_db;
INSERT INTO roles (name, description) VALUES
  ('ADMIN', 'System administrator'),
  ('AGENT', 'Lottery ticket agent'),
  ('CUSTOMER', 'Lottery customer'),
  ('AUDITOR', 'Read-only auditor');

99-grants.sql: the services don’t use the root account. They get one user that can only read and write rows, with no right to change tables:

sql
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON lottery_auth_db.*   TO 'lottery_app'@'%';
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON lottery_db.*        TO 'lottery_app'@'%';
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON lottery_ticket_db.* TO 'lottery_app'@'%';
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON lottery_draw_db.*   TO 'lottery_app'@'%';
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON lottery_winner_db.* TO 'lottery_app'@'%';
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON lottery_audit_db.*  TO 'lottery_app'@'%';

These scripts run once, the first time the database container starts with an empty volume.

Step 3: The secrets file LMS\.env

Create .env next to the compose file:

JAVA_VERSION=25
MYSQL_VERSION=9.7
MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=
APP_DB_PASSWORD=
JWT_SECRET=
AUDIT_INTERNAL_KEY=
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

Fill each value. Generate the random ones in PowerShell, one command per value:

powershell
# a password (use for MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD and APP_DB_PASSWORD)
$b = New-Object byte[] 24; [Security.Cryptography.RandomNumberGenerator]::Create().GetBytes($b); -join ($b | ForEach-Object { $_.ToString('x2') })

# the JWT secret and the audit key (must be valid base64)
$b = New-Object byte[] 48; [Security.Cryptography.RandomNumberGenerator]::Create().GetBytes($b); [Convert]::ToBase64String($b)

Take the Razorpay values from your test-mode dashboard. Use a new JWT secret: everyone just signs in again. Add these lines to your .gitignore so secrets never reach GitHub:

.env

For MYSQL_VERSION, run SELECT VERSION(); in Workbench and use the same major version. If Docker can’t find the tag, use 8.4.

Step 4: LMS\docker-compose.yml
yaml
name: lottery

# Settings shared by the six Spring services
x-java-service: &java-service
  restart: unless-stopped
  mem_limit: 512m
  depends_on:
    mysql:
      condition: service_healthy
    kafka-init:
      condition: service_completed_successfully

x-common-env: &common-env
  SPRING_DATASOURCE_USERNAME: lottery_app
  SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD: ${APP_DB_PASSWORD}
  SPRING_KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS: kafka:19092
  JWT_SECRET: ${JWT_SECRET}
  # quieter and safer than the development settings
  SPRING_JPA_SHOW_SQL: "false"
  LOGGING_LEVEL_ORG_SPRINGFRAMEWORK_SECURITY: INFO
  SPRINGDOC_API_DOCS_ENABLED: "false"
  SPRINGDOC_SWAGGER_UI_ENABLED: "false"

services:
  mysql:
    image: mysql:${MYSQL_VERSION:-9.7}
    restart: unless-stopped
    mem_limit: 768m
    command: --default-time-zone=+00:00 --character-set-server=utf8mb4 --collation-server=utf8mb4_0900_ai_ci
    environment:
      MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD: ${MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD}
      MYSQL_USER: lottery_app
      MYSQL_PASSWORD: ${APP_DB_PASSWORD}
    volumes:
      - mysql-data:/var/lib/mysql
      - ./deploy/db/init:/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d:ro
    ports:
      - "127.0.0.1:3307:3306"      # for Workbench on this PC only
    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD-SHELL", "mysqladmin ping -h 127.0.0.1 -uroot -p$$MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD --silent"]
      interval: 10s
      timeout: 5s
      retries: 30
      start_period: 40s

  kafka:
    image: apache/kafka:4.0.0
    restart: unless-stopped
    mem_limit: 1536m
    environment:
      KAFKA_NODE_ID: 1
      KAFKA_PROCESS_ROLES: broker,controller
      KAFKA_LISTENERS: INTERNAL://:19092,CONTROLLER://:9093
      KAFKA_ADVERTISED_LISTENERS: INTERNAL://kafka:19092
      KAFKA_LISTENER_SECURITY_PROTOCOL_MAP: INTERNAL:PLAINTEXT,CONTROLLER:PLAINTEXT
      KAFKA_INTER_BROKER_LISTENER_NAME: INTERNAL
      KAFKA_CONTROLLER_LISTENER_NAMES: CONTROLLER
      KAFKA_CONTROLLER_QUORUM_VOTERS: 1@kafka:9093
      KAFKA_OFFSETS_TOPIC_REPLICATION_FACTOR: 1
      KAFKA_TRANSACTION_STATE_LOG_REPLICATION_FACTOR: 1
      KAFKA_TRANSACTION_STATE_LOG_MIN_ISR: 1
      KAFKA_GROUP_INITIAL_REBALANCE_DELAY_MS: 0
      KAFKA_LOG_DIRS: /var/lib/kafka/data
    volumes:
      - kafka-data:/var/lib/kafka/data
    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD-SHELL", "/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-broker-api-versions.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:19092 > /dev/null 2>&1"]
      interval: 10s
      timeout: 10s
      retries: 30
      start_period: 30s

  # Creates the two topics once, then exits
  kafka-init:
    image: apache/kafka:4.0.0
    restart: "no"
    depends_on:
      kafka:
        condition: service_healthy
    command: >
      sh -c "/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server kafka:19092 --create --if-not-exists --topic audit-events --partitions 3 --replication-factor 1 &&
             /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server kafka:19092 --create --if-not-exists --topic audit-events.DLT --partitions 1 --replication-factor 1"

  auth-service:
    <<: *java-service
    build:
      context: ./auth-service
      args:
        JAVA_VERSION: ${JAVA_VERSION:-25}
    environment:
      <<: *common-env
      SERVER_PORT: 8081
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL: jdbc:mysql://mysql:3306/lottery_auth_db

  lottery-service:
    <<: *java-service
    build:
      context: ./lottery-service
      args:
        JAVA_VERSION: ${JAVA_VERSION:-25}
    environment:
      <<: *common-env
      SERVER_PORT: 8082
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL: jdbc:mysql://mysql:3306/lottery_db
      DRAW_SERVICE_BASE_URL: http://draw-service:8084

  ticket-service:
    <<: *java-service
    build:
      context: ./ticket-service
      args:
        JAVA_VERSION: ${JAVA_VERSION:-25}
    environment:
      <<: *common-env
      SERVER_PORT: 8083
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL: jdbc:mysql://mysql:3306/lottery_ticket_db
      LOTTERY_SERVICE_BASE_URL: http://lottery-service:8082
      DRAW_SERVICE_BASE_URL: http://draw-service:8084
      RAZORPAY_KEY_ID: ${RAZORPAY_KEY_ID}
      RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET: ${RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET}
      RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET: ${RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET}

  draw-service:
    <<: *java-service
    build:
      context: ./draw-service
      args:
        JAVA_VERSION: ${JAVA_VERSION:-25}
    environment:
      <<: *common-env
      SERVER_PORT: 8084
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL: jdbc:mysql://mysql:3306/lottery_draw_db
      LOTTERY_SERVICE_BASE_URL: http://lottery-service:8082

  winner-service:
    <<: *java-service
    build:
      context: ./winner-service
      args:
        JAVA_VERSION: ${JAVA_VERSION:-25}
    environment:
      <<: *common-env
      SERVER_PORT: 8085
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL: jdbc:mysql://mysql:3306/lottery_winner_db
      LOTTERY_SERVICE_BASE_URL: http://lottery-service:8082
      DRAW_SERVICE_BASE_URL: http://draw-service:8084
      TICKET_SERVICE_BASE_URL: http://ticket-service:8083

  audit-service:
    <<: *java-service
    build:
      context: ./audit-service
      args:
        JAVA_VERSION: ${JAVA_VERSION:-25}
    environment:
      <<: *common-env
      SERVER_PORT: 8086
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL: jdbc:mysql://mysql:3306/lottery_audit_db
      AUDIT_INTERNAL_KEY: ${AUDIT_INTERNAL_KEY}

  api-gateway:
    restart: unless-stopped
    mem_limit: 512m
    build:
      context: ./api-gateway
      args:
        JAVA_VERSION: ${JAVA_VERSION:-25}
    depends_on:
      - auth-service
      - lottery-service
      - ticket-service
      - draw-service
      - winner-service
      - audit-service
    environment:
      SERVER_PORT: 8080
      AUTH_SERVICE_URL: http://auth-service:8081
      LOTTERY_SERVICE_URL: http://lottery-service:8082
      TICKET_SERVICE_URL: http://ticket-service:8083
      DRAW_SERVICE_URL: http://draw-service:8084
      WINNER_SERVICE_URL: http://winner-service:8085
      AUDIT_SERVICE_URL: http://audit-service:8086
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"

volumes:
  mysql-data:
  kafka-data:

Your existing application.properties files stay as they are for IntelliJ. The environment variables here override them only inside Docker. If a service has a setting I haven’t listed (another *.base-url or a custom key), it will fail on startup with a clear message naming it.

Step 5: Build and start

From C:\Users\nigam\OneDrive\Desktop\LMS in PowerShell:

docker compose build
docker compose up -d
docker compose ps

The first build takes several minutes, because each service downloads its Maven dependencies. After that, only changed layers rebuild. In ps, mysql and kafka should become healthy, kafka-init should show Exited (0) (that’s correct), and everything else Up. Follow a service’s log with docker compose logs -f lottery-service.

Step 6: Create the first admin

The database is empty, so no users exist. Register one through the gateway:

powershell
Invoke-RestMethod -Method Post -Uri http://localhost:8080/api/auth/register -ContentType 'application/json' -Body '{"username":"admin","email":"admin@example.com","password":"CHOOSE-A-STRONG-PASSWORD"}'

Registration gives CUSTOMER. Add the admin role directly in the database (it prompts for your MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD):

docker compose exec mysql mysql -uroot -p lottery_auth_db -e "INSERT INTO user_roles (user_id, role_id) SELECT u.id, r.id FROM users u, roles r WHERE u.username='admin' AND r.name='ADMIN';"

Then sign in again to get a token that includes the new role.

Step 7: Test
#	Check	Expected
1	docker compose ps	mysql and kafka healthy, kafka-init exited (0), all else running
2	docker compose exec kafka /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:19092 --list	audit-events and audit-events.DLT
3	POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/login as admin	A token whose roles include ADMIN
4	Create a lottery through Postman or the frontend (still on Vite for now), header X-Correlation-Id: docker-0001	201
5	GET /api/audit/events?correlationId=docker-0001 (admin token) after a few seconds	The LOTTERY_CREATED event arrived through Kafka
6	docker compose restart kafka, make another change, wait a minute	Its event appears (it waited in the outbox)
7	docker compose down, then docker compose up -d (without -v)	You can still sign in, and the lottery exists. Data lives in volumes
8	Open http://localhost:8082 or http://localhost:3306 from the host	Not reachable (only the gateway is public)

docker compose down -v deletes the volumes, meaning all data. Don’t use -v unless you want a clean slate. Back up with docker compose exec mysql mysqldump -uroot -p --databases lottery_db ... > backup.sql before experiments.

If something fails
What you see	Likely cause
docker compose build says the Maven or JRE tag isn’t found	The Java version in .env doesn’t match an existing tag. Send me the exact message
A service restarts in a loop	docker compose logs <service> shows why. A common one is Access denied (the password in .env differs from what MySQL was created with: remove the volume with docker compose down -v, then start again)
Schema-validation: missing table	The 10-schema.sql export is missing a schema, or MySQL started before the files were in place. Run docker compose down -v, check the files, and start again
Public Key Retrieval is not allowed	Add ?allowPublicKeyRetrieval=true to that service’s SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL
A service exits with Could not resolve placeholder ...	A setting I didn’t list. The name is in the message
Gateway returns 503 for a route	That service isn’t up yet or crashed. Read its logs
port is already allocated	Something still uses 8080 (an IntelliJ run or the old stack)
What to send me

Reply with the output of docker compose ps after Step 5, and the first row of the test table that doesn’t match, with the last 30 lines of the relevant service’s logs (docker compose logs --tail 30 <service>). Never paste your .env file. When this passes, stage 2 adds the frontend (a small web server container), one public address with HTTPS, and putting the stack on a server.
